<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visoko</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Visoko is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> on the Bosna river, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its area was the political heart of the Bosnian state: the bans and kings held court nearby, Tvrtko I was crowned at Mile in 1377, and the fortress of Visoki overlooked a trading town used by Ragusan merchants. In the Ottoman period it became known for its leather industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visoko.jpg" alt="Visoko" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mhare · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visoko_from_the_Viso%C4%8Dica_hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Visoko lies between Zenica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fojnica">Fojnica</a> joins the Bosna, in a fertile basin ringed by the foothills of the Central Bosnian mountains. The name comes from the Slavic adjective visok, high, and refers to the fortress of Visoki on Visočica hill, first mentioned in a charter of Ban Tvrtko I in 1355. Before that the region around the valley was commonly called simply Bosna, and the settlement below the fortress was known as Podvisoki, below Visoki. Traces of settlement go back to the 5th millennium BCE, and at Okolište one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements of the Butmir culture in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was found, with perhaps 3,000 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daesitiates">Daesitiates</a> inhabited the area in antiquity, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> established its rule in 9 CE. The Visoko basin is considered the nucleus of the Bosnian state, which emerged around 1000. Podvisoki, Mile, Biskupići and Moštre together formed the Visoko valley, where many charters were written. Podvisoki, protected by the fortress, was long the main trade centre of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. At Mile the Rusag, the assembly, met; Tvrtko I was buried there beside his uncle Stjepan II Kotromanić, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-kulin">Ban Kulin</a>'s Plate, dated 1193, was found at Biskupići.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered the area around 1463, and the modern town took shape then, its founder being Ajas-beg, a local who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Up to 1477 he built a hammam, a school, an aqueduct, a bridge over the Bosna, a madrassa and a dervish tekke. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1878. On 11 November 1911 a fire burned down the upper town and the main street, about 450 buildings in all, and rebuilding began in 1912 in brick and tile in a traditional Bosnian style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Six national monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> stand in Visoko, among them the Old town of Visoki, Mile, the Okolište settlement and the Franciscan monastery of Saint Bonaventure. Šerefudin's White Mosque, by the architect Zlatko Ugljen, received the Aga Khan Award for Architecture in 1983. Visoko has long been known for leather, and before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a> it was the largest exporter of textiles and leather in socialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. In 2005 Semir Osmanagić claimed that Visočica and nearby hills concealed pyramids; scientific investigation found no pyramid.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Visoko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ban ruled with a council of nobles from seats around <b>Visoko</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-bosnia">Banate of Bosnia</a></li><li><blockquote>Ban Tvrtko I of the Banate of Bosnia, having won lands in western Serbia, was crowned king of the Serbs and Bosnia in 1377 at Mile near <b>Visoko</b>, claiming descent from the Nemanjić dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a></li></ul></section>
